# Static Agents

Definition: Long-running Jenkins nodes (servers/VMs) that are always connected to the Jenkins master/controller.
Setup: Installed once and permanently configured as a Jenkins node.

Characteristics:
Always available (even if no jobs are running).
Usually a fixed VM, bare-metal server, or container that is manually managed.
Requires Jenkins admin to maintain OS, dependencies, and security patches.
Wastes resources if idle.

Use Cases:
When builds need consistent environments.
For jobs with long-running, stable workloads.
Small teams with predictable build frequency.

Example: A dedicated Linux VM with Maven/Java installed, always ready to build Java apps.



# Dynamic Agents

Definition: Jenkins agents that are provisioned on-demand (spawned only when a job is triggered, then destroyed after completion).
Setup: Usually integrated with cloud providers (AWS, GCP, Azure) or container orchestrators (Kubernetes, Docker).

Characteristics:
Ephemeral – created per build and terminated afterward.
Saves costs because resources exist only when needed.
Ensures clean, isolated environments (reduces dependency conflicts).
Scales automatically with workload.

Use Cases:
Cloud-native CI/CD pipelines.
Large teams with bursty workloads.
Projects needing different environments per build.

 Example: Jenkins on Kubernetes using the Kubernetes plugin – when a job runs, Jenkins spins up a new pod as an agent, runs the build, then deletes the pod.
